    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on exploiting high-speed opportunities against pitchers with exploitable delivery profiles. **Corbin Carroll** (79.76) and **Brice Turang** (70.39) headline our lineup, both benefiting from favorable matchups against Sasaki and Matthews, respectively. Their scores are bolstered significantly by the \"Slow SP Delivery\" metric, which enhances our stolen base potential\u2014the lifeblood of our Roto strategy. **Mickey Moniak** (68.44) is another high-value play; while his raw base projection is modest, a massive +14% boost from a dynamic platoon advantage against Leahy forces him into a \"Strong\" start classification. Conversely, **Gabriel Moreno** (60.56) serves as our stable anchor at catcher, benefiting from the prime #3 spot in the order.\n\nNavigating the bench required a firm hand, particularly with **Cole Young**. Despite a respectable secondary score, his 35.72 efficiency rating fell well below our 40.0 floor, largely due to a tough draw against Rasmussen, whose 3.19 xERA suppresses the production of middle-order bats like **Max Muncy** (49.99). **Muncy** barely clung to a starting spot; while his park factor is favorable, the combination of an elite opposing pitcher and his position at the bottom of the order makes him a liability we are monitoring closely. We remain thin in the reserves as we continue to navigate the unavailability of **James Wood** and **Trevor Story**, who remain stashed on the IL.\n\nManagers should proceed with caution regarding the floor of our corner infielders. **Josh Naylor** (46.07) and **Austin Riley** (51.11) are currently operating in marginal territory. Both face sub-4.00 xERA arms, and their lack of elite speed makes them hyper-sensitive to any negative variance in batting order or park factors. While we aren't benching them yet, a single late lineup shuffle or a weather alert could see them sidelined in future rotations to protect our game caps. Stay diligent on late-breaking updates, as we need every point of efficiency from the bottom third of the order to keep pace in the SB category.",
